Subject: [PATCH] qt6 QPA genode: coalesce frames against the compositor

Register the Genode framebuffer sync signal in the Qt6 'genode' QPA
platform plugin and COALESCE frames against it, so a client can no
longer queue repaints faster than the compositor drains them - and can
never block its own event loop doing so.

Background
----------
A Genode GUI client owns exactly ONE framebuffer dataspace, mapped once;
'Framebuffer::Session' has no release or rotation API. 'refresh()' only
marks a rectangle dirty. The server composites on its own clock -
nitpicker throttles to one frame per 40 ms
(genode/repos/os/src/server/nitpicker/main.cc:561-564) - and submits one
framebuffer sync signal per composited frame (same file, :537).

The QPA plugin never registered that sync signal, so the client had no
way to learn how far behind the compositor was. Under sustained
repaints the damage backlog grows without bound while nothing leaks:
measured on Sponge OS, 335 full-document viewport repaints over 30 s
moved ZERO bytes in every component of the render chain, while the
stack delivered 11 repaints/s against a requested 30
(docs/evidence/sponge-debug-scrollstorm.md). Input therefore arrived
~3x faster than frames could be produced and the event loop stopped
draining - the field symptom "dragging the scrollbar freezes the
system".

Fix: the Wayland frame-callback pattern, not a blocking wait
-----------------------------------------------------------
At most one frame is in flight, ever. While a frame is unconsumed by
the compositor:

  * the submit does NOT blit into the buffer the compositor is reading,
  * the submit does NOT wait - it merges its damage into the pending
    region and returns immediately, so the Qt event loop keeps
    processing input throughout a storm,
  * the next acknowledged frame performs exactly ONE coalesced submit of
    everything that piled up.

    qgenodesignalproxythread.h  a 'frame_sync' proxy signal - the
                                existing entrypoint -> Qt thread idiom
                                the input path already uses
    qgenodeplatformwindow.h     the coalescing state machine, the
                                staleness guard, the telemetry counters
                                and the begin_frame()/frame_submitted()
                                contract both frame boundaries use
    qgenodeplatformwindow.cpp   sync_sigh registration, the entrypoint
                                counter, the Qt-thread acknowledgement
                                slot, the coalesced submit and the
                                bounded staleness guard
    qgenodewindowsurface.{h,cpp} the raster boundary: flush() defers the
                                blit + refresh, and the surface drops a
                                deferred submit it no longer owns
    qgenodeglcontext.cpp        the GL boundary: swapBuffers() coalesces
                                the damage notification the same way

Properties that matter for safety:

  * NEVER BLOCKS. There is no sleep, no poll loop and no usleep on the
    Qt thread anywhere in this patch. The liveness signal is an event,
    not a wait - which is the whole reason the previous revision of this
    patch was rejected (see "REJECTED: the blocking variant" below).

  * FIRST FRAME IS IMMEDIATE. Coalescing can only engage when a frame is
    already in flight, so the first paint of every window is never
    deferred.

  * ZERO COST WHEN NOTHING IS NEEDED. A client that keeps up sees the
    compositor's acknowledgement before its next submit, takes the
    one-comparison fast path, never allocates a QRegion, and logs
    nothing.

  * SELF-GATING TELEMETRY. The first coalesced frame and every 32nd one
    afterwards are logged with the cumulative counters. An application
    that never needed coalescing therefore logs NOTHING, so the absence
    of these lines is itself the "never needed" answer - this is how the
    measurement distinguishes "worked but was not needed" from "never
    engaged".

  * CANNOT WEDGE. A missed or stopped sync degrades to unpaced
    submission, never to a hang: a QTimer (STALE_FLUSH_MS, 250 ms) fires
    the coalesced submit anyway, and after STALE_STREAK_MAX consecutive
    unacknowledged flushes the window concludes the server does not
    acknowledge frames at all and disables coalescing for good. Syncing
    resuming re-enables it.

  * THREADING. The Genode signal handler runs on the component
    entrypoint and does nothing but a counter increment under a lock -
    no Qt, no blocking, no allocation. The announcement to the Qt thread
    is edge-triggered, so a burst of syncs costs at most one proxy
    wakeup. Every state field that the Qt thread owns is touched only by
    the Qt thread.

REJECTED: the blocking variant
------------------------------
An earlier revision of this patch waited (bounded, 50 x 5 ms) for the
acknowledgement at the frame boundary. Its guard was inverted, so it
never actually waited; correcting that one line made the wait real, and
the measurement collapsed:

    guard inverted (shipped) : 310 ticks / 30 s,  10 Hz, max gap  140 ms
    guard corrected          :  13 ticks / 30 s, 0.4 Hz, max gap 2657 ms

A 24x collapse, because QGenodeWindowSurface::flush() is called several
times per painted frame while the design budgeted one wait per frame,
and because blocking the Qt thread starves the very input processing
that has to keep running during a storm. DO NOT reintroduce a wait here.
Receipts and full reasoning: docs/evidence/sponge-debug-firstpaint.md
section 4 and docs/evidence/sponge-debug-scrollstorm.md section 8.

Measured effect and regression receipts:
docs/evidence/sponge-debug-scrollstorm.md; ledger row #23 in
docs/11-environment.md section 4.

Drop when upstream Genode's qt6 port registers the framebuffer sync in
the QPA platform plugin (or replaces 'refresh()' with a vsync-paced
submit).

